※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ **A floating-point number kept across a call survives it**, whatever the call
※ is.  A call to a function already compiled destroys what that function was
※ seen to write; a call to one that has not been -- itself, recursing -- is
※ taken to destroy everything its convention lets it, and that is every
※ floating-point register on all three machines: no function's prologue saves
※ one.  Here a sum carried through a loop lives across a call to the function
※ the loop is in, whose own loop writes the same register with its own sum;
※ and an answer of a whole number and a floating-point one comes back in a
※ register of each kind.

let std := ⎕import("std")

type Shape = point : void | circle : f64 | many : [Shape]

※ How many shapes there are, and what their circles add up to.
fn measured(s: &Shape) → 〈u64, f64〉:
    match s⌖:
        f64(r):
            〈1u64, r〉
        [Shape](all):
            let count: mut u64 = 1u64
            let sum: mut f64 = 0f64
            foreach &one: Shape = all:
                let m: 〈u64, f64〉 = measured(one)
                count ← count + m⟦0⟧
                sum ← sum + m⟦1⟧
            〈count, sum〉
        _:
            〈1u64, 0f64〉

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let s: Shape = Shape(.many ← [Shape(.many ← [Shape(.circle ← 2.5f64), Shape(.point ← true)]),
                                  Shape(.circle ← 0.25f64), Shape(.point ← true)])
    let p: 〈u64, f64〉 = measured(&s)
    if p⟦0⟧ ≠ 6u64: 1u6 elif p⟦1⟧ ≇ 2.75f64: 2u6 else: 0u6
